---
description: Initialize C++ members in the constructor initializer list before the body runs
globs: **/*.{h,hpp,cpp}
alwaysApply: false
---

# Initialize members before the constructor body

If the constructor body (or helpers it calls) **reads** a member, initialize that member in the constructor initializer list. Do not rely on later setters, or on the reader noticing an in-class default.

C++ does not zero members just because a constructor exists. A `bool`, `int`, or pointer with neither an in-class `=` nor an init-list entry is indeterminate; the first `if (flag_)` is undefined behavior, not a quiet `false`. Class types (`QString`, `QColor`) are safe to default-construct, but still list them so the starting state is visible next to the constructor.

In-class `= nullptr` / `= false` / `= Qt::gray` is a second line of defense, not a substitute for the init list.

```cpp
// BAD — ctor helpers read these; in-class `= false` is not a substitute
GameSetupWindow::GameSetupWindow(QWidget* parent) : QWidget(parent) {
  buildUi();
  applyUiStrings();
  updateOptionalFieldAppearance();  // reads dateEditedByUser_
}

// GOOD — flags used as edit/reentrancy guards are in the init list
GameSetupWindow::GameSetupWindow(QWidget* parent)
    : QWidget(parent),
      ignoreDateChange_(false),
      dateEditedByUser_(false) {
  buildUi();
  applyUiStrings();
  updateOptionalFieldAppearance();
}
```

Keep in-class `= nullptr` / `= -1` / `= false` as a second line of defense. Init-list order must match declaration order.

A `clear()`, `reset()`, or `import()` helper is not constructor initialization. If callers can use the object before those run, every member they read must start in a valid state from construction — not from a lifecycle method they may never call.

```cpp
// BAD — quarterPhase_ / gameStartAnchorMs_ only set in clear() or resetGameTimeState()
TagSession::TagSession(QObject* parent) : QObject(parent) {}

bool TagSession::hasGameStartAnchor() const {
  return gameStartAnchorMs_ >= 0;  // UB if constructed but clear() never ran
}

// GOOD — same defaults as clear() / resetGameTimeState(), visible in the ctor
TagSession::TagSession(QObject* parent)
    : QObject(parent),
      nextTagId_(1),
      gameStartAnchorMs_(-1),
      currentQuarterIndex_(-1),
      currentQuarterStartMs_(0),
      quarterPhase_(QuarterPhase::NotStarted) {}
```

Widget observers are `QPointer<T>`, not raw `T*`. List them as `nullptr` in the constructor initializer (declaration order) even though `buildUi()` assigns them later. `QPointer` auto-nulls if Qt deletes the child first; a raw `T*` in a row struct plus `delete` is use-after-free. Null-check `QPointer` members at use sites.

Members the constructor always assigns and never nulls (`stack_`, `workWindow_`, `tagSession_`) do not get `if (ptr_)` at use sites. A null check there is dead code and hides the real ownership rule.

```cpp
// BAD — ctor always sets stack_; it is never nulled
void showWelcomeWindow() {
  if (stack_) stack_->setCurrentWidget(welcomeWindow_);
}

// GOOD
void showWelcomeWindow() { stack_->setCurrentWidget(welcomeWindow_); }
```
